; A declaration-macro invocation INSIDE an imported module generates
; declarations in that module's namespace (MAC-EXP-16, 2026-08-15,
; where the v1 surface refused it as AX3027). What is left to refuse
; is the visibility question, and it is the same one a hand-written
; declaration answers: the template's own `pub` decides whether the
; product leaves the module.
;
; `insideModule` comes from a `pub` template and is named by this
; import's name list, so it is nameable here. Four diagnostics say
; what is not - three uses, and the import's own name list, which is
; checked against what the module exports and so reports the fourth at
; the import rather than at a use:
;
;   hiddenInside   its template omits `pub`, so it never leaves the
;                  module - the same diagnostic, with the same
;                  wording, that a private hand-written declaration
;                  draws. The module's own call to it still works,
;                  which is what makes this a visibility rule rather
;                  than a deletion
;   alsoExported   its template says `pub`, and the import's NAME
;                  LIST does not mention it. The list is applied to a
;                  generated name exactly as to a written one, which
;                  it cannot be where it is applied to everything
;                  else: the name does not exist when a module's
;                  declarations land, so import resolution records the
;                  filter and phase D asks for it
;   fromEntryMacro  generated inside DeclIn by a macro THIS file
;                   defines - and NAMED by the import's list above, so
;                   the filter cannot be what hides it and the
;                   template's missing `pub` is. The products are the
;                   invoking module's,
;                   whatever the macro's own module is - the axis the
;                   first version of this slice read backwards, which
;                   let a non-`pub` template's products out of the
;                   module in this direction and made an entry file's
;                   own generated definitions invisible in the other
;                   (tests/selfhost/388)
(import DeclIn (touch insideModule fromEntryMacro))

; a macro defined HERE and invoked inside DeclIn: its products belong
; to DeclIn, and its template says nothing about `pub`, so they stay
; there
(macro entryMk ((entryMk nm)
   (:: nm Int)
   (fn (nm) 5)))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (+ (+ (+ (+ touch insideModule) hiddenInside) alsoExported) fromEntryMacro))
